---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const casea = assertion('three-opinions-one-docket-one-judge')
const copied = assertion('a-remedy-copied-from-alabama')
const pop = assertion('a-thousand-two-hundred-and-ninety-five-to-four-hundred')
const space = assertion('fifty-four-square-feet-against-an-ordered-eighty')
const race = assertion('forty-four-point-eight-per-cent')
const count = assertion('one-hundred-and-eighty-seven-opinions')
const peak = assertion('the-nineteen-seventies-peak-is-the-law')
const where = assertion('litigated-in-other-peoples-counties')

const spec = (a: typeof pop, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const popSpec = spec(pop, 'Patients at Lima State Hospital')
const countSpec = spec(count, 'Published opinions')
---

<Article slug="fifty-four-square-feet">
  <p>
    The largest institution ever built in Allen County opened north of Lima in 1915 on 576 acres the
    state bought for $63,622, ground a county history called the poorest in the county. The record of it here ran to 1920
    and stopped. What fills the gap is a lawsuit: a federal class
    action brought on behalf of everyone confined there, which ran for seven years and left three
    published opinions.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={casea} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={copied} />
  <Gloss>
    Which is the trap this piece is built around. A remedial order is the most quotable document in
    American public life — specific, terrible, and drafted to be enforced against a future
    superintendent rather than to describe a present one. The 1974 order here forbids requiring
    patients to shine employees' shoes. Read as evidence that would be a fact about this county; it
    is a fact about Bryce Hospital in Tuscaloosa, because the court said plainly that many of its
    paragraphs were excerpted verbatim from the Alabama case it was following. What a court orders
    is a remedy. What it finds is a finding. Only the second is evidence about the ground.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>What the court found</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={pop} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="pop"
    title="A population falling before the order"
    caption="Patients at Lima State Hospital as the 1978 and 1980 opinions record them. The suit was filed in 1973; the fall from 1,295 had already happened."
    columns={['When', 'Patients']}
    rows={pop.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="pop" set:html={JSON.stringify(popSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={space} />
  <p>
    Six by nine is fifty-four square feet. The order six years earlier had required eighty, and the
    plaintiffs asked for a hundred. The court gave neither, and the distance between the number it
    ordered and the number it found is the only measurement here that either document supports.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={race} />
  <p>
    That last figure lands on a conclusion reasoned to here decades before it could be shown. Reading the
    1940 census, it noticed that Bath Township's 260 Black residents ran 204 men to 56 women, and
    wrote that those ratios describe a committed population rather than a settled one. Forty years
    later the parties to this case stipulated the number. Neither observation is evidence for the
    other. They are two views of one arrangement.
  </p>

  <h2>Counted in the reports</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={count} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="count"
    title="Which courts wrote them"
    caption="Published opinions naming Lima State Hospital, by court. Ohio's own courts account for 138 of the 187."
    columns={['Court', 'Opinions']}
    rows={count.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="count" set:html={JSON.stringify(countSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={peak} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={where} />
  <p>
    So the institution stood on this county's ground and generated its record almost entirely
    somewhere else, which is what a maximum-security hospital serving a whole state looks like from
    inside one county. The successors show up in the same reports and only from one side: Lima
    Correctional Institution no later than February 1988, Oakwood Forensic Center no later than
    August 1989, Allen Correctional Institution no later than March 1992. One name for sixty-eight
    years and then three in five, which is the shape of an institution being divided rather than of
    institutions being built. When it stopped being Lima State Hospital is not in any source
    reached here.
  </p>
</Article>
